<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ranganathaswamy Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, on an island in the Kaveri River near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, is one of the most important shrines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and among the largest functioning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple complexes in the world. Enlarged over centuries by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and Vijayanagara, it is enclosed by seven concentric walls with towering gateways.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranganathaswamy-temple-srirangam.jpg" alt="Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RUPESH MAURYA · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ranganathaswamy_temple_tiruchirappalli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple is dedicated to Ranganathar, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and his consort Ranganayaki, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>. It stands on an island between the Kaveri and Kollidam rivers, about 12 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>. According to the Sriranga Mahatmyam, the shrine's Vimanam emerged when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> performed austerities during the churning of the cosmic ocean. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> gave it to King Vibhishana, who on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> left it at the island, where it would not move; he gave it to a local king, Dharmavarma, on condition that the deity face south, as it does, in a reclining posture.</p><p>Its first builder is said to have been the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> ruler Dharmavarma; after a Kaveri flood destroyed the vimanam, the early Chola king Killivalavan is said to have rebuilt the complex. Inscriptions from the Cholas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and Vijayanagara range from the 7th to the 17th centuries. The temple took a central place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism">Sri Vaishnavism</a> in the centuries after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> (1017 to 1137) and his predecessors Nathamuni and Yamunacharya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its island site left it open to floods and to invading armies, which repeatedly used it as a camp. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> armies looted and destroyed it in a raid on the Pandyan kingdom in the early 14th century, and it was rebuilt in the late 14th century. In the 16th and 17th centuries it was fortified and gained more gopurams. The Rajagopuram, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achyuta-deva-raya">Achyuta Deva Raya</a> of Vijayanagara, was left unfinished after the empire fell in the late 16th century and reached its present 73 metres only in 1987, when the Ahobila Matha raised the funds; it was consecrated on 25 March 1987.</p><p>More than 800 inscriptions, about 640 of them on temple walls, run from the late 9th century to the 16th and are in Tamil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Telugu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> and Odia. They show that the temple also ran a free kitchen, granaries and a hospital, and financed local projects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex occupies 63 hectares and is laid out as a temple town in seven concentric enclosures, the inner five holding the shrines and the outer two homes and markets. It has 21 gopurams, 39 pavilions and many water tanks. Among its halls is the thousand-pillar mandapam of the Vijayanagara period.</p><p>Ranganathaswamy is the only one of the 108 Divya Desams praised by all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, with 247 hymns. An annual 21-day festival in the month of Margazhi attracts one million visitors. In 2017 the temple received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Asia-Pacific Award of Merit for conservation, and it is on UNESCO's tentative list for World Heritage status.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ranganathaswamy Temple, Srirangam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
